= Counterexample =

== Overview ==
A '''counterexample''' is a case that satisfies the assumptions or conditions of a general claim while making its conclusion false.

One valid counterexample is enough to refute a universal statement.

Counterexamples are fundamental tools in mathematics, logic, computer science, philosophy, and scientific reasoning because they show exactly where a generalization fails.

== Universal claims ==
Consider the claim:

<pre>
All prime numbers are odd.
</pre>

The number 2 is prime and is not odd.

Therefore 2 is a counterexample and the universal claim is false.

The logical structure is:

<pre>
Claim:      for every x, if P(x), then Q(x)
Counterexample: some a has P(a) and not Q(a)
</pre>

Formally:

<pre>
∀x (P(x) → Q(x))
</pre>

is refuted by:

<pre>
∃x (P(x) ∧ ¬Q(x))
</pre>

== Counterexample versus contrary example ==
Not every case that looks different is a counterexample.

The case must satisfy the original claim's assumptions.

Suppose a theorem applies only to continuous functions.

A discontinuous function that violates the conclusion is not a counterexample because it does not satisfy the hypothesis.

This is one reason precise definitions and scope conditions matter.

== Counterexample versus anecdote ==
An anecdote can illustrate that a generalization may be too broad, but not every anecdote formally refutes a scientific or statistical claim.

For example:

<pre>
Smoking increases cancer risk.
</pre>

is a probabilistic population-level causal claim.

One smoker who does not develop cancer is not a counterexample because the claim does not assert that every smoker develops cancer.

Understanding the logical form of the claim is therefore essential.

== Counterexamples in mathematics ==
Mathematicians use counterexamples to:
* refute conjectures;
* reveal missing assumptions;
* distinguish definitions;
* test proposed proofs;
* map boundaries between theorem classes.

A failed conjecture can often be repaired by adding the condition exposed by a counterexample.

Thus counterexamples generate knowledge, not merely destruction.

== Minimal counterexamples ==
A '''minimal counterexample''' is the smallest or simplest counterexample under some ordering.

Proof by minimal counterexample assumes a counterexample exists, chooses a minimal one, and then derives an even smaller counterexample or contradiction.

This method is related to infinite descent and induction.

== Counterexample-guided refinement ==
In computer science and formal methods, counterexamples can be generated automatically.

Model checking may return an execution trace that violates a required property.

Counterexample-guided abstraction refinement (CEGAR) repeatedly:
# checks an abstract model;
# receives a counterexample;
# determines whether it is real or caused by over-abstraction;
# refines the abstraction;
# checks again.

Counterexamples therefore support iterative verification.

== Counterexamples and falsifiability ==
[[Falsifiability]] generalizes the importance of possible refutation in empirical science.

But empirical "counterexamples" are usually not as logically clean as mathematical ones.

An anomalous observation may depend on:
* measurement;
* sampling;
* auxiliary assumptions;
* statistical variation;
* interpretation.

One empirical observation therefore does not always play the same decisive role as a formal mathematical counterexample.

== Countermodels ==
In logic, a '''countermodel''' is an interpretation/model in which the premises of an argument are true and the conclusion is false.

A countermodel proves that an argument form is invalid or that a proposed logical consequence does not hold.

Counterexamples are therefore closely related to semantic methods in logic.

== Epistemic notes ==
Counterexamples are powerful because they directly test universality. Their force depends on correctly identifying the hypotheses and logical form of the claim.

